<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni II Bentivoglio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1443">1443</a>–<a href="/years/1508">1508</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni II Bentivoglio was the effective ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> from 1463 to 1506, governing through the city's councils while keeping its formal ties to the Papal States. He was a patron of art and architecture. In 1506 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> marched on Bologna and drove him out, bringing the city under direct papal rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-ii-bentivoglio.jpg" alt="Giovanni II Bentivoglio" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ercole de' Roberti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ercole_de%27_Roberti_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giovanni was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> on 12 February 1443, the son of Annibale I Bentivoglio, chief magistrate of the commune, and Donnina Visconti. He was a small child when his father was murdered by his rival Battista Canneschi in June 1445. Bologna was then ruled by Sante I Bentivoglio, once an apprentice of the wool <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and said to be a son of Annibale's cousin Ercole. Machiavelli, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a>, tells how the Bolognese, out of goodwill toward the family, fetched Sante from Florence and let him govern until Giovanni came of age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Sante died in 1463, Giovanni made himself lord of the commune, though it was nominally a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Church under a papal legate. On 2 May 1464 he married Sante's widow, Ginevra Sforza, who bore him sixteen children and acted as his counsellor, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a> made him perpetual head of the Senate. He fought as a condottiero, and in the War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> in 1482 helped Ercole d'Este against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. In 1488 his daughter Francesca poisoned her husband, Galeotto Manfredi of Faenza; Giovanni was captured trying to put down the revolt that followed and was freed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>.</p><p>The same year he crushed a plot by the Malvezzi family, almost all of whom were hanged or exiled, and in 1501 the Marescotti met the same fate. He withstood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>, but on 7 October 1506 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> deposed and excommunicated him. As papal troops and a contingent sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a> advanced he fled, and Julius entered the city on 10 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>An attempt by his sons Annibale II and Ermes to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1507 failed, and the Bolognese destroyed his palace. He died a prisoner of Louis XII in the Castello Sforzesco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 15 February 1508. He kept a splendid court and improved the city's waterways, while the poor lived in misery. He commissioned the frescoes of Saint Cecilia's life in the Oratorio di Santa Cecilia by Francesco Francia, Lorenzo Costa the Elder and Amico Aspertini, and Costa's Bentivoglio Altarpiece in San Giacomo Maggiore.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni II Bentivoglio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pope Julius II marched on Bologna in 1506 and drove out <b>Giovanni II Bentivoglio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-bologna">Republic of Bologna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1485" aria-label="Bologna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Giovanni II Bentivoglio"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5687 3739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5687 3739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1485">What was here in 1485? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
